> ### Catastrophic climate damages
> 
> Catastrophic climate damages are based on the meta-analysis in \citet{howard2017few}, which finds that including explicit catastrophic risk adds a term proportional to squared global mean warming. We retain their central estimate and interpret it as an *expected* loss:
> \[
> \mathbb{E}\bigl[D_{\text{cat}}(\Delta T)\bigr] \;=\; \mu\,\Delta T^2, 
> \qquad \mu = 0.260,
> \]
> where $D_{\text{cat}}$ is the percentage loss in global GDP and $\Delta T$ is global mean warming (°C). The reported standard error ($\text{SE}(\mu)=0.267$) implies that the coefficient is not statistically distinguishable from zero; here it serves as a stylised representation of uncertain, low-probability, high-impact climate risks. As in \citet{rising2026comprehensive}, this is a reduced-form “black-box’’ proxy for tipping and tail risks, not a mechanistic model of specific processes.
> 
> #### Stochastic implementation
> 
> Originally, the Howard–Sterner term was applied as a deterministic expected loss. In the current implementation, we keep the same expectation but model damages as discrete catastrophic events with decay.
> 
> For each global temperature path and Monte Carlo run $r$:
> 
> 1. **Expected loss path.** Draw a run-specific coefficient
>    \[
>    \tilde{\mu}^{(r)} \sim \mathcal{N}\bigl(\mu, \text{SE}^2\bigr)
>    \]
>    and compute expected catastrophic loss
>    \[
>    e_t^{(r)} = \tilde{\mu}^{(r)}\,\Delta T_t^2, \qquad t \ge 2025.
>    \]
> 
> 2. **Event size and decay.** Assume each event causes an immediate loss of $L = 25\%$ of GDP and that past losses decay at rate $\delta \in [0,1]$. Let $C_t^{(r)}$ be realised loss (percent of GDP):
>    \[
>    C_t^{(r)} = L\,\mathbf{1}\{U_t^{(r)} < p_t^{(r)}\} + \delta\,C_{t-1}^{(r)},
>    \]
>    with $U_t^{(r)} \sim \text{Uniform}(0,1)$.
> 
> 3. **Matching the expectation.** For each scenario, we choose $\delta$ and implied annual event probabilities $p_t^{(r)}$ so that
>    \[
>    \mathbb{E}\bigl[C_t^{(r)}\bigr] = e_t^{(r)} \quad\Rightarrow\quad
>    p_t^{(r)} = \frac{e_t^{(r)} - \delta\,e_{t-1}^{(r)}}{L},
>    \]
>    and numerically select the largest $\delta \in [0,1]$ for which all $p_t^{(r)} \ge 0$. We then simulate the time series $C_t^{(r)}$ using these probabilities.
> 
> This produces, for each scenario $s$, run $r$, and year $t$, a simulated catastrophic loss $C_{t}^{(r,s)}$ (percent of GDP) over 2025–2100, stored in `damage.cata`. Figure~\ref{fig:cat_model} shows that the ensemble mean of $C_t^{(r,s)}$ closely tracks the Howard–Sterner expected loss $\mu \Delta T_t^2$, while introducing stochastic, persistent shock realizations.
> 
> \begin{figure}[htbp]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{channels/catastrophic/model.pdf}
>   \caption{Simulated catastrophic damages (solid) versus expected quadratic loss (dashed), by scenario. Lines show mean percent loss in global GDP across Monte Carlo runs.}
>   \label{fig:cat_model}
> \end{figure}
> 
> #### Summary indicators and dataset
> 
> From `damage.cata` we construct summary indicators:
> 
> 1. **Expected annual damages (EAD).** For each year $t=2025,\dots,2075$:
>    - “Most Likely”: all runs flagged as likely.
>    - “High Risk”: all runs under SSP3-7.0.
>    
>    For each case we compute the ensemble mean and 5th–95th percentiles:
>    \[
>    \mu_{s,t} = \mathbb{E}_r\bigl[C_t^{(r,s)}\bigr], \quad
>    \text{CI}_{5,s,t}, \quad \text{CI}_{95,s,t},
>    \]
>    and label these rows as `measure = "EAD"`.
> 
> 2. **Extreme outcomes.** For the “High Risk’’ (SSP3-7.0) case and benchmark years $t_0 \in \{2030, 2040, 2050\}$, we consider the 10‑year window $[t_0, t_0+9]$. For each run $r$ we take
>    \[
>    M_{r,t_0} = \max_{t \in [t_0, t_0+9]} C_t^{(r,\text{ssp}370)},
>    \]
>    then summarise $\mu_{t_0}$, $\text{CI}_{5,t_0}$, and $\text{CI}_{95,t_0}$ across runs; these rows are labelled `measure = "Extreme"`.
> 
> All results are saved in `channels/catastrophic/dataset.csv` with columns:
> \[
> \{\text{scenario}, \text{year}, \text{measure}, \mu, \text{CI}_{5}, \text{CI}_{95}\},
> \]
> where all values are percent of global GDP.
> 
> Figure~\ref{fig:cat_dataset} plots the resulting series: shaded ribbons show uncertainty in EAD for “Most Likely’’ and “High Risk’’ cases; red error bars show uncertainty in the decade‑maximum “Extreme’’ outcomes for the High Risk case.
> 
> \begin{figure}[htbp]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{channels/catastrophic/dataset.pdf}
>   \caption{Summary catastrophic damage indicators. Lines and shaded bands show mean and 5th–95th percentiles of expected annual damages (EAD) by scenario. Red error bars show the mean and 5th–95th percentiles of 10‑year maximum catastrophic losses in 2030, 2040, and 2050 under the High Risk scenario.}
>   \label{fig:cat_dataset}
> \end{figure}
